      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before becoming Hayes, the area was called Dugas Pasture, named after a cattle grazer from Andrus Cove who discovered the fertile pasture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayes%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1904 the Louisiana Western Railroad ran through Hayes, and Toliver Hayes sold the depot site and 40 acres to the company, shaping the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hayes%2C_Louisiana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Lorrain Bridge, built in the 19th century, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places for its unique design and construction.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
